Z||000022||JohnnyK||05-07-2001||04:58 PM||||Lobe: I totally respect that man, but there are two sides to that story.  If me and my buddies were playing mini golf and started shouting obsenities (we do sometimes.. we suck at minigolf..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and you came over and talked to us in a way that I would talk "Hey... Just wondering if you guys could stop swearing as  have an 8 year old over there. Sorry guys, but you know?.." I would be VERY helpful. However, if you came over and "How about you teenagers stop swearing because I have a young kid over there" in an angry voice, I would not be too helpful. If you came off as a dick, I would try to make your game as upsetting as possible... But that would only be in an extreme case where the father is quite rude..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||DRH||05-07-2001||05:55 PM||||JohnnyK,<P>I kinda agree about someone asking nicely EXCEPT in this case... You wouldn't only be messing up the dad's day, but the kids too... It wouldn't be their fault their dad was rude.<P>Just one of those things you realize as you move into the crazy dad years. The innocence of childhood doesn't last nearly long enough as it is... It's worth protecting whether it's your kids or someone else's... That's why crazy dads get rude sometimes... And that's why sometimes you should let them get by with it...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||frontal lobe||05-07-2001||11:28 PM||||JohnnyK, I think you could tell from the way I typed it that I tried to be extremely polite about it, because I wouldn't blame someone at all for reacting if I got in their face about it.<P>Tweek, I just wanted to say again that I wasn't personally offended by the picture and would hope people would have thicker skin and not be so easily offended like our society has become.  Live and let live.  I was just trying to point out that all the people out there seeing what we do are not adults yet.  But certainly in this all adult forum, we can take stuff and not be offended.  So...I think you should repost the picture so others can see what we were talking about.  But again, that is just an opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Cheever2||05-08-2001||01:40 AM||||The posting of the photo here was not the problem.  I once considered leaving the Forum over decency issues, but have recently obtained software which is very effective at limiting what my kids get into.  They know I like the Forum, but I don't like the thought of not wanting them to see some of the stuff I see (and would rather not).  But the photo being here was not the problem I had.<P>I have been chomping at the bit to get home from work and make my rebuttal.  If I'm the only one who believes this way, then I will be greatly discouraged.  And if some do, but don't speak up, that is truly sad.  JohnnyK, I continue to disagree with you.  I do not mean to attack you personally, but all those for whom your words represent.  If you met me in person, you would find me to be a calm, humble (not modest, and that's a flaw of mine) , usually quiet guy.  I would respect you, and go to great lengths to remain friends.  But... (I know, mine is always the biggest "but" in the Forum)... your argument does not sit well with me.<P>Take the mini-golfing dad, for example.  He shouldn't <B><I>have</I></B> to talk to them about their language.  They should already have the decency, the manners, the self-control to hold their tongue in public.<P>And <B><I>they</I></B> don't have to be polite and respectful, but he does?  That's how you say the encounter should take place. <P>Besides, once they have used those words (without first considering who is within hearing range), it's really too late.  The damage has already been done.  The kids have already heard it.<P>You can offend me all you want, I'm a grown up.  And if it were a place they had not paid to attend, they could just walk away (though again, they shouldn't have to).  But if I've paid money to be there, I'm not going to teach my kids to be cowards and walk away from doing what's right.  I'd rather it be the foul-mouth punks who leave, and in an ambulance if necessary.  You & your buddies want to be offensive, you can do it someplace else, like in a hospital room, and I'd be happy to send you there if you offend my family.  My kids would learn three things from that: (1)-It's not acceptable to talk like that in public; (2)-Dad will take care of his family; and (3)-don't mess with Dad.  I have not yet had to put this into practice, but I would if the situation were that severe.  I've come close many times, usually in traffic.<P>Yeah, something like that may get me shot someday, but I'm going to defend what's right or die trying.  I'd be honored for my kids to learn <B><I>that</I></B> from my example.  But if I'm going, I intend to take someone with me.<P>Sounds militant, I know.  Actually, I will take a LOT of crap.  But if somebody is not going to give me the minimal respect I deserve as a human being, I'm certainly not obligated to have any for them.  I understand how non-Christians don't want religion crammed down their throat.  In fact, after the evolution topics, I promised some Forum members that I will henceforth stay out of any religious debate here.  You can surely see that I in turn don't want anyone's vulgarity pushed down MY throat.  You can dig your own hole if you want, but don't throw the dirt on ME.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000026||JohnnyK||05-08-2001||01:51 AM||||I didn't say the kids should be rude either. But sometimes, while having fun, people get rowdy and shout things out. But i think it would be VERY wrong for a kids dad to beat up some teenagers who were being annoying and "punkish". In fact, I would say that is very immature. That would just turn into a "My dad will beat you up". I am glad that my dad has never touched anyone in his entire life basically. Also, as much as you aren't modest, I think you'd have a tough time beating up a teenager.. I don't think it would be too tough to knock out some 400 year old father..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But (yes, I have a but too) you say you are a Christian and you love deceny, morals, etc.. Yet, I still can't understand why you would even THINK of beating someone up to the point of putting them in the hospital. I believe that (and I'm sure most people will agree) that is a LOT worse than using vulgar language in front of children, etc. That doesn't SOUND very Christian to me.. Beating someone up over a disagreement.. Anyways, I'm done for the moment..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||Cheever2||05-08-2001||02:11 AM||||Okay, I must admit you now have some worthy points.  My reactions are OFTEN worse than what prompted them, another flaw.  Guess I'm saying that I'm human and have my limits.  This is one of the few examples of something that will set me off.  The only others I know of are: being unjustly accused, seeing an indefensible person being tormented, and certain traffic situations.  I don't "shelter" my kids, not even close, but I do expect a standard of public decency around children & women, a standard which does not change in spite of a changing social moral (women themselves are often the perpetrators of public indecency more now than just a few decades ago).  To me, right is right, regardless of whether people's <B>opinions</B> of "right" changes.  This whole matter goes right to the heart of the Golden Rule, and it drives me crazy that people of all faiths agree with it, but few live by it.  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...I think you'd have a tough time beating up a teenager.. I don't think it would be too tough to knock out some 400 year old father..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE>  You would be surprised.  Remember, people who are the best at something are often those you'd least expect.<P><BR>||||reg||1
