Z||000036||frontal lobe||05-08-2001||10:25 AM||||A STUN GUN!!!  Now that's a story I want to hear.  We need a thread:  "Who all has been assaulted by a stun gun?"!!<P>firstfiero, yes you have the RIGHT to your freedom of speech.  I have no problem with that.  The problem I have is with your attitude which apparently would be "I am so selfish that I have MY rights and I don't care in any way how MY exercising MY rights would affect any other human being at all because I have MY rights.  And MY rights are what's important in this world so if anyone doesn't like what MY rights allow ME to do, well then steer clear, buddy, because I have MY rights".  I don't know if that accurately represented your attitude because it's sometimes hard to accurately get what someone is saying in print.  But if it is, then there are large number of you around our society, and my personal decision is to not try to demand MY rights to not let you infringe on mine, but to steer clear.  You may not find others so gracious.  From your posts, I am guessing you wouldn't care either way because you know how to handle yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000037||EXHIBITER||05-08-2001||10:57 AM||||AMEN.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Cheever2||05-08-2001||11:23 AM||||I was expecting that "freedom of speech" phrase to be used several posts ago.  Just as I started to believe in the sanity and reason of the people here, now somebody brings that up.  Not that I detest true freedom of speech, just the use of it as a banner to hide behind, when "freedom to offend and do whatever I want" is what is really being sought.  Unfortunately, that battle cry is being heard and surrendered to.  Not by me, mister.<P>The United States continues to stand on two things: (1) Liberty, and (2) Justice.  Now, think about this for just a few minutes, will ya... in some situations the two are in conflict.  Sometimes there has to be a decision as to which of those should be the predominate principle.  A person may have the freedom (liberty) to do something, but it may not be right (just) under the circumstances.  Conversely, if a situation is strictly enforced by the law (justice), it may unduly affect someone's rights (liberty).  Our courts and jurors occassionally must choose between the two.  Sometimes the wrong choice is made, bad laws are written, and it's unfortunate but there is a proper and legal way to remedy it (which is why I believe rioting should be fiercely dealt with).<P>I will obey the law of the land, even when I don't agree, UNLESS it conflicts with the few principles I hold dear enough to never relinquish.  Therefore, I could in fact go to jail someday for retaliating against those who intentionally offend for their own enjoyment, when it involves my kids.  So, when I do retaliate and risk so much, I will make it worth it and be brutal.  It will be overkill, but I will take care of business and finish the job.  It won't be pretty.<P>Those of you who habitually and inconsiderately use offensive language in public: please realize there is a reaction that you may not see.  The people who seem to not notice or care, well, they really do.  It makes the hair stand up on the back of their neck.  They want to do something about it, but it's everywhere and the world seems hopeless, so they don't.  At least I feel that way.  And there's just so much of it I can take.  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000039||EXHIBITER||05-08-2001||11:36 AM||||Right on Cheever!||||reg||14
Z||000040||buds||05-08-2001||01:03 PM||||great picture tweek <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'd have no problem showing that to my kids(if I had any)cause its a joke! ha ha funny stuff||||reg||1
Z||000041||JohnnyK||05-08-2001||01:35 PM||||Damn.. I miss a lot of action when I go to sleep for 12 hours (sheesh).. Anyways, I make it a note never to use the whole freedom of speech thing, because I'm not familiar with the actual, specific law.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Cheever, I'm sure we WILL meet, and I'll hold you to that drink (second round can be on me), even if it is at a Fiero Meet and I'm driving (hopefully) a classic Stang..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> But I think you've got to relax with vulgar language. It's just language. The planet is not going down the crapper because of it. People grow out of it. I'm getting a little afraid of your whole plan to "End it fast and brutally" or something like that. You don't wear a Stone Cold 3:16 shirt around do you??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000042||firstfiero||05-08-2001||07:02 PM||||   No I don't care. It doesn't bother me. I'm not saying I'm one of these people and just don't get upset about because they have the right to do and say what they want. You have the right to go up and ask for your money back or ask managment to have them removed. Again I say I don't ever act like that in public...I just respect other peoples right to do as they choose. <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000043||firstfiero||05-08-2001||07:33 PM||||     Cheever I was just thinking some more on what you said. It makes no sense. Are you saying that to teach your children one little lesson it's worth them not having a father. "Well kids I know it sucks that I can only see you threw this glass but gee I sure taught you not to curse in public didn't I." makes no sense dude. by the way I have talked to you several times on the chat line. You seem like a hell of a nice guy and I hope you don't have any hard feeling about are disagreement here. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000044||Cheever2||05-09-2001||02:16 AM||||firstfiero, I thank you for your kind word.  And no hard feeling at all, I really mean that.  It's just like you called it - a disagreement.  <P>Let's review my prior remarks.  First, I told of my dismay... er, irritation...  no, indignation at those who enjoy proving to the world how radically they can demonstrate the garbage between their ears.  I don't believe, JohnnyK, that "people grow out of it".  Some do, but I see little evidence that it's the norm.  And, yes, I actually do believe the constant barrage of it is chipping away at our civility and our ideas of what society is & can become.  You don't agree?  I respect that.  Then came the example (in frontal lobe's case, real experience) of the mini-golf guys using F words in every sentence without regard to the audience.  Here's where I started to come unglued.<P>In the original discussion of the T-shirt, I was simply disappointed... okay, upset.  But not to the point of outrage.  In the latter discussion, please remember we're not talking about the run-of-the-mill (just where did that expression come from anyway?) outburst of 1 or 2 curse words which barely raise an eyebrow on even the most conservative of people.  My outrage would come in a very extreme situation, when people not only have NO respect for others, but make an unreasonable effort to disrupt my family's Kodak moments, which we have every right to pursue without malicious interference.<P>ray b, are you keeping up?  I'm saying that I'm not a dangerous Christian walking around with a lit fuse all the time.  Somebody gets in my face, I'm going to move THEIR face, that's all, as long as my face wasn't where it shouldn't be.  And really it's not even about me, it's my defense of my family, that's the part of society I am responsible for.  You are responsible for your part, and it's not my business if you ignore your responsibility.  <P>So, firstfiero, I read your last post early during my shift at work and had all evening to think about it.  And yes I seriously contemplated what you said.  These matters are too important to let my pride get in the way if I'm wrong.  I asked myself, would I REALLY do those things?  I imagined as best I could that it were actually happening, right now, what are you going to do, Dad?  As much as I visualized the result of backing down from it, I cannot tell you that I would.  I'll continue to think this over, believe me, you ask a good question.  All I know is if it were to happen today, I'm pretty sure my reaction would be as I described earlier.  Again, we're talking about a very serious (to me) situation, not an ordinary one, and I wouldn't take it lying down.  I'm not sorry for that.  There is a point of righteous indignation, and until I'm otherwise enlightened, that's the line.<P>Don't fear getting to know me in some car show or Fiero event.  I'm all smiles, and do whatever it takes to get along.  My 13 year old son will be with me at the FieroFest in Oklahoma City, and I just ask that you treat him like a 13 year old all-American boy.  Really, it would take a LOT to disrupt our sensibilities (hasn't happened yet!).  We know it's the real world.  We just have a bright vision of what we want the world to be.  Is that so bad?<BR>||||reg||1
